html:div.html:details:
Answer Compute (OPs/s), bandwidth (bytes/s), and total memory (bytes). Everything an accelerator can do is rationed by one of the three, and the first two give the only two times worth computing:
T_{\mathrm {math}} = \frac {\text {computation FLOPs}}{\text {accelerator FLOPs/s}}, \qquad T_{\mathrm {comms}} = \frac {\text {communication bytes}}{\text {bandwidth (bytes/s)}}
"Comms" means either HBM \leftrightarrow compute cores within a chip, or chip \leftrightarrow chip over ICI / DCN / PCIe. Same formula either way — only the bandwidth in the denominator changes, which is why the same model covers a TPU Architecture memory stall and a Tensor Parallelism all-reduce.

Answer \max (T_{\mathrm {math}}, T_{\mathrm {comms}}) \le T \le T_{\mathrm {math}} + T_{\mathrm {comms}}
The lower bound assumes communication is perfectly overlapped with compute; the upper bound assumes no overlap at all. Since T_{\mathrm {math}} + T_{\mathrm {comms}} \le 2 \max (T_{\mathrm {math}}, T_{\mathrm {comms}}), reasoning only about the max costs you at most 2\times — which is what makes the roofline worth trusting before any code exists.
An op is compute-bound when T_{\mathrm {math}} > T_{\mathrm {comms}}: the FLOPs are the ceiling, and full Model FLOP Utilization (MFU) is the thing to chase. It is comms-bound (equivalently memory- or bandwidth-bound) when the reverse holds and the ALUs sit idle waiting on data.

Answer
  Arithmetic intensity is FLOPs per byte moved:

  \mathrm {Intensity}(\text {algorithm}) = \frac {\text {computation FLOPs}}{\text {communication bytes}}

  The compute-bound test falls straight out of it:

  T_{\mathrm {math}} > T_{\mathrm {comms}} \iff  \frac {\text {FLOPs}}{\text {FLOPs/s}} > \frac {\text {bytes}}{\text {bytes/s}} \iff  \frac {\text {FLOPs}}{\text {bytes}} > \frac {\text {FLOPs/s}}{\text {bytes/s}}

  that is, \mathrm {Intensity}(\text {algorithm}) > \mathrm {Intensity}(\text {accelerator}). The hardware's intensity is its ridge point — peak FLOPs/s divided by bandwidth. For the TPU v5e TensorCore (TPU) MXU that is 1.97 \times  10^{14} / 8.2 \times  10^{11} \approx  240 FLOPs/byte. Below the ridge you ride the sloped bandwidth roof; above it you sit on the flat peak-FLOPs roof.

Answer
  2\ \text {bytes} \times  200\mathrm {e}9 = 400\mathrm {e}9 \text { bytes} \;\longrightarrow \; \frac {12.5\mathrm {e}9 \text { bytes/chip}}{1.23\mathrm {e}12 \text { B/s}} \approx  10\ \mathrm {ms}

  It is a bound because every decode step has to re-read all the parameters from HBM into the MXU, so no step can beat the weight-load time. At small batch it is close to achievable, too: decode is HBM-bandwidth-bound, not FLOPs-bound, which is the whole reason batching pays.

